Work queue (in this order, per Andres - new items get appended at the end unless he
says otherwise). Reordered 2026-08-02.

Tier 1 - quick wins (small, isolated changes):

- RESOLVED: session.php polling looked "stuck" on mobile (iOS Safari specifically)
  even at a 1s interval, with updates arriving in bursts and a manual refresh not
  helping either - root cause found and fixed 2026-08-02, confirmed live. Two
  layered issues, in order of how the investigation went:
  (1) A session's sidecar recorded claude_session_id once at spawn and never
  updated it, so after /clear/compact/resume/fork the app kept reading an
  abandoned transcript file forever - fixed via a SessionStart hook, see the
  Tier 3 item below for the full writeup.
  (2) Even once reading the right file, start_app_session() (called by
  session_detail.php/session_history.php/sessions_list.php/quota.php to keep a
  polling tab's CSRF token alive) sets PHP's session-cache-limiter headers
  (Cache-Control: private, max-age=60) - fine for session.php's own HTML page
  (that's deliberately short, see the bfcache fix below), wrong on these JSON
  polling endpoints: it let a browser legally serve a stale cached response to
  the exact same fetch() URL for up to 60s, no matter how fast the page polled -
  and a plain refresh didn't help either, since the reload's own fetch() calls
  hit that same cache entry. iOS Safari does this in practice (confirmed via
  curl showing the leaked header, then fixed); other browsers may or may not,
  depending how aggressively they cache fetch() GETs. Fixed by each of those 4
  endpoints sending its own Cache-Control: no-store right after
  start_app_session(), overriding the session limiter's header.

- host-agent/lib/Transcript.php (summarize_tool_use()): reformat tool_use/MCP call
  summaries away from "ToolName(arg)" - drop the parentheses, use a structured
  "tool: X / Params: / - Y / - Z" listing (all params, not just the one primary
  arg currently shown), collapsing to a single line with no expand/collapse
  affordance if the whole thing fits on one line (same as the existing trivial-block
  rule for render_collapsible_block()).
- session.php: Andres reports history/prompt bubble text overflows somewhere -
  needs more specifics (which bubble/block type, screenshot if possible) to
  narrow down.
- session.php: mystery solved - the "type custom answer" box wasn't actually
  closing/losing its draft on a poll (already confirmed preserved), but the
  textarea loses focus when renderBlockedSection() rebuilds the DOM, so typing
  continuously gets silently interrupted and needs a re-tap/re-click to resume -
  that's what read as "closing". Fix: after the rebuild-and-restore in
  renderBlockedSection(), if the freetext textarea had focus before the rebuild,
  re-focus the new one (and restore cursor position) rather than just its value.
- session.php: Andres reports command/prompt-answer text now appears centered
  instead of left-aligned after the no-wrap/scroll change (see below) shipped.
  Checked live: computed text-align is "start" (left) all the way up the ancestor
  chain (<pre>/<details>/wrapping divs) for both the transcript-history path and
  the blocked-prompt-card path, in Chromium via Playwright - could not reproduce.
  Possibly the SAME stale-page-cache bug already fixed below (Andres's browser
  could have been looking at HTML/JS from before some earlier fix) - worth a fresh
  look now that a genuinely fresh load is confirmed working, before assuming it's a
  genuine mobile-Safari-specific rendering quirk.
- RESOLVED: "unexpected response" alert when answering a prompt or sending a
  compose message, worst on the iOS "Add to Home Screen" launch (no refresh
  gesture, can stay resumed in the same JS session for hours). Two real bugs
  found and fixed together: (1) session.php et al sent Cache-Control:
  private, max-age=10800 (PHP's 180-minute session default), so a browser
  could serve a 3-hour-stale copy of the page on a plain navigation - fixed
  via session_cache_expire(1) in Auth.php's start_app_session(), pinning
  max-age to 1 minute while still avoiding Cache-Control: no-store (preserves
  the original bfcache fix for the Home Assistant iOS webview embed). (2) The
  4 read-only polling endpoints (session_detail.php, session_history.php,
  sessions_list.php, quota.php) never called start_app_session(), so a tab
  left open just watching (never sending/answering) could silently outlive
  PHP's session GC - the resulting stale-CSRF-token POST came back 403 with a
  plain-text body ("Rejected: missing or invalid CSRF token."), which
  fetch().then(r => r.json()) can't parse, surfacing as a bare "Unexpected
  response" with no indication it was actually a CSRF/session issue. Fixed by
  having all 4 join the session too, keeping it alive for as long as the page
  is open and polling. Also improved the error message itself either way
  (parseJsonResponse() in session.php/index.php reports the real status code
  + a body snippet instead of a bare "Unexpected response"). Confirmed fixed
  live: Andres deleted and re-added the iOS home-screen icon (forcing a fresh
  load past the stale disk cache) and it now works.
- session.php: pull quota info from the tmux session itself and refresh it on every
  poll, instead of the current custom script/endpoint (quota.php) which is a bit slow.
- session.php: in the compose textarea (and free-text reply box), flip Enter/Shift+Enter
  - plain Enter should insert a newline, only the Send button should submit. Currently
  the opposite (Enter submits, Shift+Enter newlines).
